2. RINNIE'S MORNING

Don't open on Dusty. Open on Rinnie.

Morning in Aethres. The healer's house — small, warm, clean. Rinnie sits by the window tending a plant that's doing better than she is. Her hands are steady today. Good day. She talks to the plant about her brother — not worry, not sadness, just information delivered sideways. "He's probably already lost. He doesn't do well with big buildings. Too many rooms, nowhere to hide."

The healer checks on her. Rinnie performs wellness — sits straighter, smiles on cue, deflects. The healer isn't fooled but accepts the performance because Rinnie needs to believe it works.

She writes Dusty a letter. Short. Asks about things she knows the answer to just to give him something to respond to. Mentions the plant. Doesn't mention the trembling in her hands last night or the hours she spent staring at the ceiling. Signs it with a small drawing of a leaf — because a leaf is alive and growing and that's what she wants him to think she is.

Cut to Dusty on his first day — but now every decision he makes carries a different weight because we've already met the girl it's all for. The reader knows what Rinnie hides. Dusty doesn't. The gap between what he imagines she's doing and what she's actually doing is the engine.

The arc follows both: Rinnie's quiet days in Aethres — the garden, the healer, the letters, the bad days she edits before they reach him — intercut with Dusty's first days at Mythrandis. Two halves of one sacrifice. The arc closes with Dusty receiving the letter, reading it in his room, and smiling at the leaf. He doesn't know what it cost her to draw it. We do.

WHAT IT DOES: Establishes the emotional core before the world. Makes the reader protective of Rinnie before they know the academy. Every political manuever, every noble slight, every risk Dusty navigates — the reader already understands the stakes. Not expulsion. Rinnie.


