3. CIEL ON THE ROOF

Open on Ciel. The day before orientation. Everyone else arrives tomorrow.

She's sitting on the highest point of the central tower — a spot no student is supposed to reach, which means nothing to someone who can fly. Below her, the academy prepares. Maids move through corridors. Faculty adjust schedules. Gardeners tend the paths the new arrivals will walk.

She's watched this fifteen times. Fifteen cohorts. The same preparations, the same anticipation, the same arrivals — temporary, loud, irrelevant. She catalogues the activity below the way she catalogues everything: duration, frequency, deviation from pattern. "The eastern garden has been watered fourteen minutes earlier than baseline. The head gardener is anxious."

A Maid places tea on the parapet beside her. Ciel didn't hear her arrive. She never does. The tea has an extra pastry — not on anyone else's tray. Ciel notes the caloric addition. Does not note the love.

The cohort arrives. She watches from above. Carriages. Entourages. One boy walks alone across the bridge with one bag. She measures his stride — efficient, no wasted motion, scanning. Files it. Moves on. Doesn't know she'll remember that measurement.

The arc is one day — Ciel's last day of solitude before the new students arrive and the noise starts again. Through her eyes the reader sees the academy as a machine, beautiful and impersonal, and the girl who has been inside it so long she can't tell the difference between herself and its architecture. The arc ends at curfew. She returns to her room. Six hours until morning. She counts them. She doesn't know she's counting because she's lonely. She thinks she's counting because counting is what you do.

WHAT IT DOES: Opens on isolation, not action. Establishes Ciel as the character the reader will ache for before a single word of dialogue is spoken. Her clinical voice narrating a world she doesn't belong to. When the other characters arrive — loud, messy, political, human — the contrast is devastating. And buried in the opening: a boy with one bag, measured once and filed. A seed the reader won't recognize until it blooms.


