5. THE FAILED SAINTESS AND THE EMPTY TABLE

Open on Auriel. Second year. She already knows how this works.

Morning. She gets dressed — bright, deliberate, armor made of color and energy. Walks to breakfast glowing. Not the divine glow — the personality. She fills hallways with warmth and noise because silence is where people decide to leave.

The dining hall. She picks a table. Sits down. Begins eating. Two students approach — see her — course correct. A third makes eye contact, almost sits, then spots Rariel three tables over and recalibrates. The mathematics of exclusion executed so smoothly it barely registers as cruelty. Auriel keeps eating. Keeps smiling. The smile is the same one from five minutes ago, but the warmth underneath has cooled a fraction of a degree that only she can feel.

The arc follows her through one day in the social machine. Classes where she participates too eagerly. Study groups she isn't invited to. Tea at four — the worst hour, the hour that makes isolation a performance art — where she sits alone and radiates warmth to an empty table. Rariel passes. Their eyes meet. Rariel looks away first. That's new. Rariel used to look through her. Looking away means she still sees her, and seeing her means it costs something to keep walking.

Then: the new students arrive for orientation. Auriel watches from a balcony. One of them — Dusty, though she doesn't know his name yet — sits alone in the dining hall. Not excluded. Alone by choice. The difference is visible to someone who's become an expert at distinguishing the two. She almost goes down. Hesitates. The half-second pause that didn't exist a year ago. She goes anyway, because the sun doesn't know how to not shine, even when shining just means burning alone.

WHAT IT DOES: Opens on the damage already in progress. The reader meets Auriel mid-wound, mid-dimming, and understands the cost of isolation through someone built entirely for connection. The empty table is the image that carries the arc — divine power, enough to level a cathedral, and no one will sit with her. When the new students arrive, the reader wants Auriel to find someone. Not because the plot demands it. Because it hurts to watch.


