6. THE RESEARCHER'S MORNING

Open on Valia's workspace. Pure chaos — papers, instruments, half-eaten food, three unrelated experiments running simultaneously. The Maid assigned to her for the day enters, surveys the damage, says nothing, and begins working around it with surgical precision. She knows not to touch the stack by the window. She knows the food on the left table is yesterday's. She knows the experiment near the door is actually three experiments that Valia forgot she combined.

Valia arrives — late, talking to herself, mid-thought on step six of a process she hasn't explained step one of. She grabs the wrong notebook, realizes, doesn't fix it, starts a tangent. The Maid places tea exactly where Valia's hand will reach without looking. Valia drinks without noticing.

Today is different. Today the boy arrives.

The arc follows Valia trying to prepare for Dusty's first day — she promised herself she'd be organized, available, a proper mentor. Instead she gets derailed by a conductivity reading that can't be right, argues with herself about whether to check it or meet the incoming students, checks it, loses two hours, remembers Dusty, panics, runs — literally runs, robes flapping, knocking something over in the corridor — and arrives at the orientation hall too late. He's already been processed, assigned, absorbed into the system.

She finds him later. The meeting is awkward. She starts mid-thought. He listens, trying to determine if she's a threat. She doesn't notice his evaluation because she's already three thoughts past introductions. But somewhere in the conversation she says one thing — one fragment about his mana, about what she noticed, about why it mattered — and it lands. Not because she was trying. Because she was honest without knowing it.

Arc closes on her walking back to her lab, having knocked over a different thing on the way out, already thinking about three other things. She doesn't know she just changed someone's life. She doesn't know she changed it the first time either. She just keeps working.

WHAT IT DOES: Opens on warmth and absurdity. A comedic tone that lets the reader breathe before the heavier material. Valia's chaos is endearing and reveals the academy's dysfunction — a brilliant woman dismissed, maintained out of guilt, right about everything and heard by no one. Through her the reader meets the institution's cruelty from the other side: not the student it grinds, but the faculty it wastes. And the meeting with Dusty — messy, accidental, genuine — contrasts every calculated interaction the academy is about to throw at him.


