7. THE MAID SERVES TEA

Open on no one important. Open on a Maid.

Four o'clock. The Maid walks through corridors that aren't on any map. Behind the walls, through passages hidden by buttons and levers she presses without breaking stride. She carries a silver tray. The tea is perfect. It is always perfect.

She emerges into the main hall and the world changes — students everywhere, the social machinery of Mythrandis in full operation. She moves through it like water through stone. Invisible. Observing everything.

Through her eyes, the reader sees the academy's first day from a position no character occupies — above it. She serves Brycendar's table and notes the way his entourage arranges itself: Serwyn too close, Graeven watching everyone, Lethane not watching anything, Rashten trying not to break the porcelain. She serves Aivriel and notes the queen who drinks tea as though ratifying a trade agreement. She serves the empty space around Auriel and notes the geometry of avoidance — who moved, who looked, who pretended not to see.

She delivers a tray to a small room in the central tower. Ciel is sitting by the window. The Maid places the tea, adds the extra pastry that isn't on anyone else's tray, and leaves. Ciel does not look up. The Maid does not expect her to.

She passes a boy in the corridor — new, lottery mark, one bag, checking exits. She notes him. Files nothing. There is nothing to file yet.

She returns through the hidden corridor. Behind the walls, she removes her gloves. Her hands are scarred — old scars, deep ones, the kind that come from decades of something that wasn't cleaning. She puts the gloves back on. Adjusts the tray. Straightens her posture. Emerges into another hallway as though she has always been there and has never been anywhere else.

The arc is one afternoon. Tea time. Through the eyes of the one group that sees everything and says nothing. No plot is advanced. No conflict ignited. The reader simply watches the watchers — and understands that the real power at Mythrandis has never been in the towers.

WHAT IT DOES: Establishes the Maid Corp as the invisible spine of the story. Creates mystery without exposition. Every character is introduced through observation rather than participation — the reader sees them the way the institution sees them, and the Maid's silence says more than any faculty briefing could. The scarred hands are a promise: there is more here, and it will wait.


