8. THE LAST NIGHT IN THE GREYS

Don't open at the academy. Open in the Greys the night before Dusty leaves.

Rain. The kind that makes the gutters overflow and the rats come up from underground. Dusty is in the room — the last room, the one he found three months ago when the old place flooded. It's not a room. It's a gap between two buildings with a tarp over it and a door someone threw away that he propped against the entrance.

Rinnie is asleep. Wrapped in every piece of fabric he owns, curled on the pallet he built from crates. Her breathing is steady. Good night. He's memorizing the sound because tomorrow he won't hear it.

He doesn't sleep. He sits with his back against the wall, mana steady in his hands — the black glow with the silver outline, just enough warmth to keep the chill off her. He's done this a thousand nights. This is the last one.

The arc is retrospective. Through this final night, Dusty thinks — not in exposition, in images. The plague that took their parents. The years of scrounging. The fights he won and the ones he ran from. Rinnie's first bad day, when she couldn't stand, and he carried her for six hours to find a healer who'd look at a Greys kid. The healer who turned them away. The one who didn't. The day Valia appeared — strange, scattered, incomprehensible — and his certainty that it was a trap, followed by the slow, grudging realization that some people are exactly what they appear to be.

Dawn. He wakes Rinnie. They walk through streets she's known her entire short life. The carriage — academy-provided, the nicest thing either of them has ever touched — waits at the district's edge. He puts her in it. She puts on the brave face. He sees through it. He puts on his own. She sees through it too. Neither of them says so.

She goes to Aethres. He goes to Mythrandis. The carriage splits at a crossroad, and for the first time in his life, he can't see her.

WHAT IT DOES: Makes the reader earn the academy. Before the marble and the gardens and the politics, this is where Dusty comes from. The squalor is not backstory — it's the opening image. When the reader finally sees Mythrandis through his eyes, every beautiful surface carries the memory of what he left, and every noble who looks through him is measuring a person they have never been qualified to measure.


