10. THE CATACOMBS BREATHE

Open underground. No characters the reader knows. Not yet.

Darkness. The kind that has weight. Stone older than the academy, older than the nation that built the academy, older than the histories that recorded the nation. Carved walls — symbols no living language claims. Air that moves when it shouldn't, as though the tunnels themselves are breathing.

Something lives here. Not the sealed demonic god — deeper, older, scattered. Mana spirits, the ancient ones, the ones who remember. They drift through the dark in forms chosen centuries ago — a fox of pale blue light the size of a house. A serpent of green flame coiled around a column that predates written language. A bird of violet mana perched where no ceiling should be, watching with eyes that have seen the Demon-kin walk in kindness and then burn in human fire.

They feel the academy above them. Five thousand mana signatures, each one a heartbeat. Most are noise — the same noise, year after year, generation after generation. But today, in the new arrivals, there is something. A flame that doesn't deplete. A mana reserve that acts like it isn't a reserve at all — like it's a source.

The oldest spirit — the fox, the one who remembers the Demon-kin — lifts its head. It hasn't moved in decades. It moves now. Not toward the surface. Toward the deeper tunnels, the ones even the spirits avoid, where the sealed thing presses against its prison and the walls carry heat that stone shouldn't hold.

It pauses. Listens. The sealed thing is listening too.

The fox turns away. Settles. Waits. It has waited centuries for less interesting things.

Above, the academy carries on. Students unpack. Faculty prepare. A Maid delivers tea. A boy checks the exits in his new room and doesn't unpack because unpacking means trusting. He doesn't know what stirs beneath him. He doesn't know the oldest things in the world just felt him arrive.

WHAT IT DOES: Opens on the mythology, not the academy. Establishes that the story is bigger than school politics — that there are ancient powers, ancient debts, and something sealed in the dark that the entire institution is built on top of. Dusty is introduced not as a student but as a signal, detected by beings old enough to remember a genocide. The reader knows before any character does that this is not just a school story. The ground itself is watching.


